1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,789
Aarav switches on his
grandfather's old valve radio.

2
00:00:03,789 --> 00:00:05,826
For a few seconds, nothing.

3
00:00:05,826 --> 00:00:07,607
Then the music fades in.

4
00:00:07,957 --> 00:00:10,566
His phone plays the same song instantly.

5
00:00:10,566 --> 00:00:11,622
Why the wait?

6
00:00:12,122 --> 00:00:14,572
Inside that radio are vacuum tubes.

7
00:00:14,572 --> 00:00:17,946
And a vacuum tube cannot
work until it gets hot.

8
00:00:18,296 --> 00:00:20,806
To see why, let's look inside the metal.

9
00:00:21,456 --> 00:00:23,676
A metal is full of free electrons.

10
00:00:23,676 --> 00:00:26,945
They wander inside, but the
surface holds them back.

11
00:00:27,295 --> 00:00:30,719
To escape, an electron needs
a minimum amount of energy.

12
00:00:30,719 --> 00:00:32,782
This is called the work function.

13
00:00:33,482 --> 00:00:37,560
For tungsten, it is about four
point five electron volts.

14
00:00:37,560 --> 00:00:42,251
An electron volt is a tiny energy
unit, made for single electrons.

15
00:00:42,951 --> 00:00:44,394
Now heat the metal.

16
00:00:44,394 --> 00:00:46,960
The electrons jiggle faster and faster.

17
00:00:47,310 --> 00:00:50,725
Some gain enough energy to
jump out of the surface.

18
00:00:50,725 --> 00:00:52,623
This is thermionic emission.

19
00:00:52,623 --> 00:00:55,406
Thermionic simply means given off by heat.

20
00:00:56,056 --> 00:01:01,060
In nineteen oh four, John Ambrose Fleming
used this idea to build the vacuum

21
00:01:01,060 --> 00:01:01,960
diode.

22
00:01:02,048 --> 00:01:04,348
A heated cathode gives off electrons.

23
00:01:04,348 --> 00:01:08,734
A metal plate, the anode, sits across
an empty space, with all the air

24
00:01:08,734 --> 00:01:09,634
pumped out.

25
00:01:09,945 --> 00:01:14,250
Let's put it on the breadboard, with
an alternating supply and a resistor.

26
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:18,567
When the anode is positive,
it pulls the electrons across.

27
00:01:18,567 --> 00:01:19,670
Current flows.

28
00:01:20,020 --> 00:01:23,234
When the anode turns
negative, it pushes them back.

29
00:01:23,234 --> 00:01:24,533
No current at all.

30
00:01:25,183 --> 00:01:26,400
Watch the scope.

31
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:28,437
The input swings up and down.

32
00:01:28,437 --> 00:01:30,929
The output keeps only the top halves.

33
00:01:31,279 --> 00:01:33,812
Current flows in one direction only.

34
00:01:33,812 --> 00:01:38,428
Turning alternating current into
one-way current is called rectification.

35
00:01:39,078 --> 00:01:43,549
In nineteen oh six, Lee de Forest
added a wire mesh, the grid, between

36
00:01:43,549 --> 00:01:45,312
the cathode and the anode.

37
00:01:45,662 --> 00:01:50,027
A small voltage on the grid
controls a big current to the anode.

38
00:01:50,027 --> 00:01:52,349
This triode could amplify signals.

39
00:01:52,999 --> 00:01:54,975
But tubes had real problems.

40
00:01:54,975 --> 00:01:58,421
The heater wasted power
and needed time to warm up.

41
00:01:58,771 --> 00:02:00,961
The anode needed hundreds of volts.

42
00:02:00,961 --> 00:02:05,693
The glass was big and fragile, and the
heater burned out, like an old bulb.

43
00:02:06,343 --> 00:02:09,164
The answer was a
semiconductor, like silicon.

44
00:02:09,514 --> 00:02:13,708
A semiconductor conducts better than
an insulator, but not as well as a

45
00:02:13,708 --> 00:02:14,608
metal.

46
00:02:14,629 --> 00:02:19,633
Each silicon atom has four outer electrons,
and shares them with four neighbours.

47
00:02:20,283 --> 00:02:22,261
Add a few phosphorus atoms.

48
00:02:22,261 --> 00:02:27,351
Phosphorus has five outer electrons,
so one is left over and free to move.

49
00:02:27,351 --> 00:02:30,546
This is n-type silicon,
with negative carriers.

50
00:02:31,196 --> 00:02:32,607
Add boron instead.

51
00:02:32,607 --> 00:02:36,700
Boron has only three, so one
bond is missing an electron.

52
00:02:36,700 --> 00:02:40,751
That gap is a hole, and it
acts like a positive charge.

53
00:02:40,751 --> 00:02:42,437
This is p-type silicon.

54
00:02:43,087 --> 00:02:46,466
Adding tiny impurities on
purpose is called doping.

55
00:02:47,116 --> 00:02:50,131
Now join a p-type piece
to an n-type piece.

56
00:02:50,131 --> 00:02:51,883
This is a p-n junction.

57
00:02:52,233 --> 00:02:56,039
Near the joint, free electrons
cross over and fill holes.

58
00:02:56,039 --> 00:02:59,394
A thin layer with no free
carriers is left behind.

59
00:02:59,394 --> 00:03:01,346
This is the depletion layer.

60
00:03:01,996 --> 00:03:06,602
It creates a small barrier, about
zero point seven volts for silicon.

61
00:03:07,252 --> 00:03:10,746
Connect the p side to plus
and the n side to minus.

62
00:03:10,746 --> 00:03:12,275
This is forward bias.

63
00:03:12,625 --> 00:03:17,037
Above about zero point seven volts,
the barrier gives way, and current

64
00:03:17,037 --> 00:03:17,945
rises fast.

65
00:03:17,945 --> 00:03:20,639
The scope draws the curve as it happens.

66
00:03:21,289 --> 00:03:22,925
Now flip the battery.

67
00:03:22,925 --> 00:03:24,901
The p side goes to minus.

68
00:03:24,901 --> 00:03:26,537
This is reverse bias.

69
00:03:26,887 --> 00:03:30,603
The depletion layer widens,
and almost no current flows.

70
00:03:30,603 --> 00:03:34,026
Just like the vacuum diode,
it is a one-way valve.

71
00:03:34,676 --> 00:03:38,010
A vacuum tube needs heat
to free its electrons.

72
00:03:38,010 --> 00:03:40,749
A p-n junction needs no heater at all.

73
00:03:41,549 --> 00:03:42,828
Worked example.

74
00:03:42,828 --> 00:03:48,021
A silicon diode and a one kilo ohm
resistor are connected to a five volt

75
00:03:48,021 --> 00:03:50,064
supply, in forward bias.

76
00:03:50,414 --> 00:03:51,765
What current flows?

77
00:03:52,115 --> 00:03:55,040
The diode takes about
zero point seven volts.

78
00:03:55,040 --> 00:03:59,015
The rest, four point three
volts, falls across the resistor.

79
00:03:59,715 --> 00:04:02,957
Current equals voltage
divided by resistance.

80
00:04:02,957 --> 00:04:06,801
Four point three volts
divided by one thousand ohms.

81
00:04:07,501 --> 00:04:09,874
That is four point three milliamperes.

82
00:04:09,874 --> 00:04:13,278
Flip the diode, and the
current drops to almost zero.

83
00:04:13,928 --> 00:04:16,157
So why did semiconductors win?

84
00:04:16,507 --> 00:04:18,081
A tube needs a heater.

85
00:04:18,081 --> 00:04:21,304
A semiconductor needs none,
so it starts instantly.

86
00:04:21,654 --> 00:04:23,941
A tube needs hundreds of volts.

87
00:04:23,941 --> 00:04:26,938
A semiconductor works on just a few volts.

88
00:04:27,288 --> 00:04:29,579
A tube is a fragile glass bulb.

89
00:04:29,579 --> 00:04:33,129
A semiconductor is tiny,
solid and lasts for years.

90
00:04:33,129 --> 00:04:36,680
In nineteen forty seven,
it gave us the transistor.

91
00:04:37,330 --> 00:04:38,795
Now, the exam trap.

92
00:04:39,145 --> 00:04:43,859
Many students connect forward bias the
wrong way, with the p side to minus.

93
00:04:44,209 --> 00:04:48,167
Forward bias means p side
to plus, n side to minus.

94
00:04:48,167 --> 00:04:49,200
P to plus.

95
00:04:49,200 --> 00:04:50,857
Remember the two Ps.

96
00:04:51,507 --> 00:04:53,115
Where is this asked?

97
00:04:53,115 --> 00:04:57,993
PUC board papers ask you to compare
tubes with semiconductors, or explain

98
00:04:57,993 --> 00:04:58,893
doping.

99
00:04:58,706 --> 00:05:03,487
JEE Main asks for the current in
diode circuits, as in February twenty

100
00:05:03,487 --> 00:05:04,480
twenty one.

101
00:05:05,130 --> 00:05:06,030
Try this.

102
00:05:06,014 --> 00:05:10,898
Two silicon diodes, both forward
biased, are in series with a two kilo

103
00:05:10,898 --> 00:05:13,722
ohm resistor across a six volt battery.

104
00:05:13,722 --> 00:05:15,329
What is the current?

105
00:05:20,729 --> 00:05:21,629
C.

106
00:05:21,190 --> 00:05:23,370
Two point three milliamperes.

107
00:05:23,720 --> 00:05:27,446
Each silicon diode takes
about zero point seven volts.

108
00:05:27,446 --> 00:05:30,254
Two of them take one point four volts.

109
00:05:30,604 --> 00:05:33,851
That leaves four point six
volts across the resistor.

110
00:05:34,201 --> 00:05:39,669
Four point six divided by two thousand
ohms gives two point three milliamperes.

111
00:05:40,319 --> 00:05:41,840
Screenshot this card.

112
00:05:45,040 --> 00:05:49,517
Next, integrated circuits,
microprocessors and microcontrollers.

113
00:05:49,517 --> 00:05:53,368
How do billions of transistors
fit on one tiny chip?
